  "descriptions": {
    "preferred": "This brilliant cultivar has a rich, bright reddish purple spring color which becomes deeper as summer advances. In late summer the leaves become red bronze, but in deep shade are greenish. Fall tones are brilliant crimson and scarlet. The seven-lobed leaves are of a firm texture, measuring 5-7 cm long and up to 9 cm wide. The leaf base is roughly truncate. The lobes separate to about halfway to the leaf base. Each lobe is ovate-acuminate, with the end tapering to a sharp point. The margins are evenly and finely serrated. The red petioles are slender but not weak and 3-4 cm long. This upright, widespreading tree reaches 6-7 m high in 25 years. The young plants grow rapidly, then slow Corvallis matsumurae -variegated This tall-growing, red-leaved cultivar has occasional variegation. After observing it at Maplewood for six years, I named and registered it as"
  },
